  m_Name: CavityPumpData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Progressive Cavity Pump
  engineDescription: A Cavity Pump, commonly known as a Progressive Cavity Pump,
    is a positive-displacement pump used to transfer liquids, viscous fluids, slurries,
    and fluids containing suspended solids through the progressive movement of sealed
    cavities. Its main components include the rotor, stator, casing, drive shaft,
    coupling rod, inlet chamber, outlet chamber, seals, and bearings. During operation,
    a helical rotor rotates inside an elastomeric stator with a specially designed
    internal geometry. This creates a series of sealed cavities between the rotor
    and stator. As the rotor rotates, these cavities progress continuously from the
    suction side toward the discharge side, carrying the fluid with minimal pulsation.
    The fluid is then discharged at the required pressure. Progressive cavity pumps
    are widely used in wastewater treatment, food processing, chemical industries,
    petroleum applications, sludge handling, and industrial fluid transfer. From
    an engineering perspective, understanding rotor-stator geometry, cavity formation,
    volumetric efficiency, slip, elastomer compatibility, pressure generation, lubrication,
    wear, and dry-running limitations is essential for reliable pump operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
